<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tay Son dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1778 CE – 1802 CE · capital Phu Xuan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A peasant-rooted Vietnamese dynasty of three brothers who toppled both the Nguyen and Trinh lords and routed a Qing invasion at Dong Da in 1789.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1771 the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-nhac">Nguyen Nhac</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quang-trung">Nguyen Hue</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lu">Nguyen Lu</a> of Tay Son village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binh-dinh-province">Binh Dinh</a> rose against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nguyen-lords">Nguyen lords</a>, attracting peasants, merchants and highland peoples with promises to redistribute wealth. In 1778 Nguyen Nhac proclaimed himself emperor at Do Ban, near the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijaya-champa">Vijaya</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quang-trung">Nguyen Hue</a> defeated a Siamese army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rach-gam-xoai-mut">Rach Gam-Xoai Mut</a> in 1785 and overthrew the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/trinh-lords">Trinh lords</a> in 1786. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing</a> invaded to restore the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-le-dynasty">Later Le dynasty</a>, he declared himself emperor Quang Trung and destroyed them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ngoc-hoi-dong-da">Dong Da</a> in 1789. He promoted chu nom as an official script, reformed land registers and planned a new capital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nghe-an-province">Nghe An</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quang-trung">Quang Trung's</a> death in 1792 left the throne to his young son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canh-thinh">Canh Thinh</a>, and the brothers' realms quarrelled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gia-long">Nguyen Anh</a>, backed by French volunteers and southern supporters, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hue">Phu Xuan</a> in 1801 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thang Long</a> in 1802, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tay-son-dynasty.jpg" alt="Tay Son dynasty" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: DannamEmpire · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vietnam_in_late_18th_century_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1778 CE – 1802 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Phu Xuan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nguyen Nhac, Quang Trung, Canh Thinh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Confucianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Vietnamese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Vietnam</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nhà Tây Sơn</dd></dl></aside></div>

